          "Ask how the provider handles material that has little resale value and what happens if inspection changes acceptance. Responsible processing has to account for the whole accepted batch, including fractions that do not command an attractive price. Verify the relevant receiving entity and facility scope rather than relying on an environmental slogan. In India, covered e-waste and waste batteries have distinct regulatory frameworks, and a coordinated service should explain both routes where relevant. A collection receipt supports traceability but does not establish that every downstream process has finished. Agree what later evidence is available and avoid demanding a fabricated recovery percentage to make a report look complete.",
          "For an organisation, value also includes recovering storage space, protecting information and closing asset records without unresolved ownership disputes. These benefits should not be converted into unsupported monetary or carbon figures. Track concrete outcomes such as accepted quantities, documented destinations, devices approved for reuse and unresolved exceptions. Review procurement and maintenance choices that created avoidable waste, including incompatible accessories, repair barriers or over-purchasing spare equipment. Recycling is one part of a better lifecycle decision, not compensation for buying equipment unnecessarily. A useful review asks how the next purchase and retirement can be managed more effectively while keeping safety and real operational needs central.",
