          "Plan for the actual building and weather. Covered loading access is valuable during Kerala rains, but stairways, exits and damp basements should not become default storage areas. Arrange trained help for heavy equipment and do not ask residents to disconnect installed electrical systems or dismantle appliances to fit a lift. Disclose water exposure and battery damage before movement rather than hiding them in packaging. If a battery is hot, smoking or hissing, move people away and contact emergency services instead of treating it as a routine collection item. A provider's scheduled visit is not a substitute for an emergency response or a condition-specific handling assessment.",
          "At collection, match the accepted items to the list and retain an acknowledgement identifying the collector and intended destination. Keep business asset identifiers private and separate any promised data-treatment evidence from the general receipt. Report the event honestly: equipment was collected for an identified route, with any later verified outcomes added when available. Avoid claiming every kilogram was recycled immediately or every household achieved a fixed environmental saving. Follow up on unaccepted items and unresolved records so the effort does not end with a cleaner lobby but an unclear downstream chain. Repeated collections should improve acceptance instructions and ownership controls based on practical problems encountered, not merely aim for a larger photographable pile.",
